// TEST: Range Over An Unresolved Extent Name (rejects)
// ERROR: BL3999
// ERROR-CONTAINS: is not known at compile time
// The other half of 239. A bare name in a `SymIdx` base slot reads as an extent
// EXPRESSION; when it resolves to neither a value nor an index type, there is
// nothing to iterate. `range<>` is virtual -- it materializes no object -- so
// codegen takes every bound and output extent from the slot's own Extent, and a
// symbolic placeholder has nothing to give: what reached the C++ was
// `__range0.extents[0]`, a reference to the operand variable a virtual range
// deliberately never declares. The program died in g++, naming a
// compiler-internal, for a mistake the front end could see.
//
// Refused at the range seam instead -- the same door that already refuses a
// wreath slot for the same reason ("accurate about the failure, wrong about
// whose fault it is"). Deliberately NOT refused there: `__`-prefixed internal
// sentinels, the bad-spec error markers, and `"?"` (lowerExtentExpr's fallback
// for an extent that is an EXPRESSION rather than an unknown name --
// `range<Idx<arity(args)>>` in arity/015 lands there and is unrolled into an
// array literal before codegen, so refusing it would reject a working program).
let A: Array<Float64 like Idx<3>> = [1.0, 2.0, 3.0]
let bad = method_for(range<SymIdx<2, Bogus>>) <@> lambda(i, j) -> A(i) * A(j) |> compute
